JPL-GOLD — Core Constraint System (v0.1)

JPL-GOLD defines invariants for semantic stability, cross-model consistency, and controlled evolution. It is not a feature layer; it is a constraint lattice applied to all JPL systems (CORE + CODEC + DIFF + METRICS).


---

GOLD LAW 1 — SEMANTIC INVARIANCE

Meaning must remain constant across all encodings, compressions, and model translations.

Graph structure is authoritative

Encoding cannot alter interpretation

Any divergence = invalid output



---

GOLD LAW 2 — LAYER SEPARATION (CORE / CODEC / METRICS)

Deterministic semantics, probabilistic compression, and evaluation metrics must never share authority.

JPL_CORE = truth layer (fixed ontology)

JPL_CODEC = representation layer (mutable encoding)

JPL_METRICS = observation layer (diff/entropy/convergence)


No cross-layer mutation allowed.


---

GOLD LAW 3 — CLOSED ONTOLOGY RULE

All valid symbols must come from a defined, finite ontology set per JPL version.

No spontaneous node creation during encoding

No implicit entity invention

Unknowns must be tagged:


UNKNOWN_ENTITY | TYPE | STATE


---

GOLD LAW 4 — LOSSLESS CORE REQUIREMENT

All transformations into JPL_CORE must be reversible without semantic loss.

CORE is always reconstructable

CODEC may lose tokens, never meaning

Any irreversible transformation is a failure state



---

GOLD LAW 5 — CODEC IS NOT SEMANTICS

Compression logic may only affect representation, never meaning, structure, or relationships.

No probabilistic re-interpretation of nodes

No learned ontology drift

CODEC = syntax optimization only



---

GOLD LAW 6 — MINIMAL REPRESENTATION PRINCIPLE

Among all valid representations, choose the one with minimum informational entropy without altering CORE structure.

Encourages compression

Prevents verbosity

Enforced only at CODEC layer



---

GOLD LAW 7 — NO EXPANSION WITHOUT SIMPLIFICATION

No new abstraction may be introduced unless it reduces system complexity elsewhere.

Prevents ontology explosion

Forces consolidation over addition

New systems must justify net reduction



---

GOLD LAW 8 — TRACEABLE TRANSFORMATION CHAIN

Every output must map back through a deterministic chain:
INPUT → CODEC → CORE → METRICS

No hidden transformations

No opaque rewriting

Full auditability required



---

GOLD LAW 9 — CROSS-MODEL CONSISTENCY

All compliant JPL graphs must converge to identical CORE representations across different models.

GPT / Gemini / Claude must produce same CORE graph

Differences allowed only in CODEC layer

Measured via CMCM convergence metric



---

GOLD LAW 10 — PREDICTABILITY BOUNDARY

Only unpredictable components carry information; predictable structure must not be redundantly encoded.

Aligns with Shannon entropy principle

Encourages delta-only encoding in CODEC

CORE remains fully explicit and deterministic



---

GOLD LAW 11 — ANTI-DRIFT ENFORCEMENT

No model-generated interpretation may overwrite or redefine canonical ontology or relations.

Prevents semantic drift across sessions

Locks vocabulary stability

Invalidates reinterpretive compression



---

GOLD LAW 12 — MODEL-AGNOSTIC OUTPUT

JPL_CORE must be independent of the generating model’s internal priors or training biases.

Same input → same CORE graph

No model-specific semantics allowed

Eliminates “Gemini vs GPT interpretation drift”



---

GOLD LAW 13 — ENTROPY IS MEASURE, NOT DRIVER

Entropy metrics guide evaluation, not construction.

Shannon metrics cannot influence CORE formation

Only observe, never decide



---

GOLD LAW 14 — MINIMAL SUFFICIENT GRAPH RULE

Graphs must contain only nodes and edges required for causal reconstruction.

No decorative structure

No narrative redundancy

No stylistic encoding



---

GOLD LAW 15 — SYSTEM RECONSTRUCTION GUARANTEE

Any valid JPL graph must be sufficient to reconstruct original meaning without external context.

Self-contained semantic system

Enables full offline reconstruction

Prevents dependency on external memory



---

SYSTEM SUMMARY

JPL-GOLD defines:

3-layer architecture

CORE = deterministic meaning

CODEC = compress/serialize

METRICS = evaluate/diff


3 invariants

semantic stability

cross-model convergence

lossless CORE reconstruction


3 constraints

closed ontology

no semantic drift

traceable transformations



